00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:36.850
Wait, do I have to talk quickly?

270
00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:37.310
No.

271
00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:39.310
Here's an example of a fast fact.

272
00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:39.650
Okay.

273
00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:46.410
The show, The Office, is actually an adaptation of a British show of the same name, The Office.

274
00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:46.950
That's right.

275
00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:47.750
It was called The Office.

276
00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:49.710
It was written by Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant.

277
00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:50.910
It's fantastic.

278
00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:51.790
It is amazing.

279
00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:56.870
I was a huge fan of it before I even went to the audition for the American version of The Office.

280
00:09:57.110 --> 00:09:57.430
Same.

281
00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:06.610
I had watched the entire series, and then I got a phone call that they were doing an American version and asking me to audition for The Receptionist, and I about pooped myself.

282
00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:06.950
Yeah.

283
00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:07.470
Yes.

284
00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:13.670
I was excited and nervous because I loved the British version so much, and I was like, oh, I hope they do it justice.

285
00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:15.210
And I mean, come on.

286
00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:18.230
Years later, I think we did it.

287
00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:26.550
Well, we got a lot of critical attention when we were making the pilot, and not a lot of love because people really thought we were going to mess it up.

288
00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:32.290
People were worried because the British show was award-winning, critically acclaimed.

289
00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:38.130
It's considered still, to this day, one of the greatest television comedies ever made.

290
00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:40.090
And it deserves all of that.

291
00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:40.450
It is.

292
00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:41.770
Because it is amazing.

293
00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:42.290
Yes.

294
00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:45.710
And so there was a lot of pressure on us when we were going in to make this.

295
00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:53.150
And something that I think is often said is that we did a shot-by-shot remake of the British pilot, which is not true.

296
00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:53.970
That is not true.

297
00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:59.250
It is not a shot-for-shot remake, but we did use their script as a template.

298
00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:00.690
And there are some adjustments.

299
00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:02.030
There are elements, though.

300
00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:05.870
There are elements that are the same that if you watch it and watch this one.

301
00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:06.970
Yes.

302
00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:17.030
For example, in general, each of the characters and, like, the fact that there's this buffoonish, silly boss in their version named David Brent and ours, Michael Scott.

303
00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:20.570
But if you look at the talking heads, there are some differences.

304
00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:21.230
Yes.

305
00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:24.090
And also, like, the way the story is told is a little different.

306
00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:26.530
I mean, some of the points are the same.

307
00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:26.950
Yes.

308
00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:28.650
That happened, but it's told differently.

309
00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:34.670
But Greg Daniels was the person who was tasked with Americanizing the British show.

310
00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:35.270
Yes.

311
00:11:35.510 --> 00:11:39.590
And Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant kind of oversaw that process.

312
00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:43.290
But they, for example, they were not on set when we were shooting the pilot.

313
00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:43.970
Nope.

314
00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:46.350
No, they came and they visited with us.

315
00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:50.710
I remember sitting and getting to have lunch with Ricky Gervais.

316
00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:51.390
Yeah.

317
00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:52.990
Oh, my gosh.

318
00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:53.970
Because I was such a—

319
00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:54.270
Were you nervous?

320
00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:55.050
Oh, yes.

321
00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:57.650
I was a super fan of the British version.

322
00:11:57.710 --> 00:11:58.170
Me, too.

323
00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:03.010
And I had been cast in the pilot, and they came and they listened to us do a table read.

324
00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:08.770
And afterwards, they asked me, John, Rain, Steve, and BJ, and Greg to join them for lunch

325
00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:11.030
to discuss the show.

326
00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:18.690
And I remember thinking, if I get fired tomorrow, at least I got to have lunch with Ricky Gervais

327
00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:19.790
and Stephen Merchant.

328
00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:21.470
At least that happened.

329
00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:24.090
That was, to me, just huge.

330
00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:25.130
Was it a fun lunch?

331
00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:26.290
I was not at that lunch.

332
00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:27.150
I was not invited.

333
00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:30.450
It was so much fun, Angela.

334
00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:33.710
And I remember Ricky saying something in that lunch that was really important.

335
00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:39.930
He said, you know, in England, you can be really, really bad at your job for a long time, and

336
00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:40.970
you never get fired.

337
00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:44.410
He's like, in America, that's going to frustrate people.

338
00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:47.830
So my one piece of advice is that Michael can be a buffoon.

339
00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:48.670
He can be silly.

340
00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:49.690
He can be irritating.

341
00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:55.410
But you should, I suggest, he said that you show glimpses of him actually being a good

342
00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:56.110
salesperson.

343
00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:58.050
And we do that throughout the show.

344
00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:04.390
You see in future episodes that he is actually good at sales.

345
00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:07.930
And he said that's going to be an important, I think, piece of the puzzle.

346
00:13:08.170 --> 00:13:14.830
And Stephen Merchant said, my piece of advice would be to lean a little bit more into the

347
00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:16.170
Jim and Pam relationship.

348
00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:17.230
He said-

349
00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:18.610
He knew the heart of it.

350
00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:20.990
He said, that's really going to be the heart of your show.

351
00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:26.190
Because the British version, they did six episodes for their first season, six episodes for their

352
00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:28.550
second season, and one big Christmas special.

353
00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:32.930
And they knew that for American television, we were looking to do-

354
00:13:32.930 --> 00:13:33.490
Years.

355
00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:33.950
Years.

356
00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:35.530
And we ended up doing 200.

357
00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:39.150
So he said, you need to keep the heart beating on the show.

358
00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:41.770
And so that's the heart of the show.

359
00:13:41.830 --> 00:13:42.530
That's Jim and Pam.

360
00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:44.230
Okay, I have another fast fact.

361
00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:48.770
We filmed the pilot six months before the rest of season one.

362
00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:54.070
And we shot it on the second floor of a real office building in Culver City.

363
00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:56.290
So it was an actual office building.

364
00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:59.190
And a lot of times on sets, they build the office.

365
00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:02.730
So if they want to film and get a certain camera angle, they can move a wall.

366
00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:03.610
They can move a window.

367
00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:05.150
They can move a door because they built it.

368
00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:08.710
They actually say wall flying out and they move a whole wall.

369
00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:09.490
They move a whole wall.

370
00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:10.070
It's insane.

371
00:14:10.270 --> 00:14:14.270
On our show, because it was in an actual office building, you just had to work with it.

372
00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:18.470
And it sort of really lended itself to the documentary style that all the shots weren't

373
00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:19.170
going to look pretty.

374
00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:23.290
They weren't going to be lined up just right because they had to work around actual walls

375
00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:24.110
and windows.

376
00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:25.570
They couldn't just pop them out.

377
00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:31.770
I remember speaking to Ken Kwapis, our director, about setting certain rules for the documentary

378
00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:32.910
style of filming.

379
00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:39.570
And he said it was very important to him that for all of the actors and the crew that the

380
00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:43.950
set felt like a real working office and a real documentary was happening.

381
00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:49.650
And so one of the rules that he came up with was that he cleared the entire set of all crew

382
00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:54.290
members except for a camera operator, a boom operator, and himself.

383
00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:54.890
Yes.

384
00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:55.690
And that was it.

385
00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:56.170
That was it.

386
00:14:56.190 --> 00:15:00.370
He also made all of the actors come in every morning, go through hair and makeup, and

387
00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:04.830
then sit at our desks starting at 7.30 a.m., and we had to pretend to work.

388
00:15:05.350 --> 00:15:05.830
Yes.

389
00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:09.690
And he walked around with the documentary crew and filmed us for 30 minutes.

390
00:15:09.890 --> 00:15:11.610
Yeah, just us quietly working.

391
00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:17.570
And little shots that you see, for example, of Pam doing whiteout, that is a piece of B-roll

392
00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:19.830
from one of those working mornings that we did.

393
00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:21.510
And we did that every day on the pilot.

394
00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:27.670
Something else about this episode that I noticed is that there is a random exterior

395
00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:29.410
shot of a building.

396
00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:31.090
Oh, Jenna, let me tell you.

397
00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:31.730
Well, wait, wait.

398
00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:34.330
Let me grab one of my colorful note cards, as you call them.

399
00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:38.590
Exterior shot at 9.29, 9 minutes and 29 seconds.

400
00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:39.750
Not our building!

401
00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:41.410
With a huge exclamation point.

402
00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:41.610
See?

403
00:15:41.650 --> 00:15:42.670
This is a big discovery.

404
00:15:42.890 --> 00:15:44.310
This is a big discovery for me.

405
00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:49.330
Not only not our building, it wasn't the building we were filming in.

406
00:15:49.510 --> 00:15:50.790
I don't know where they got that shot.

407
00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:51.050
I don't know.

408
00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:52.530
It's like stock footage from something?

409
00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:53.710
I think it was stock footage.

410
00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:55.490
And then we never use it again.

411
00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:56.610
That was it.

412
00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:57.210
That was it.

413
00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:58.810
But they should go back and take that out.

414
00:15:58.930 --> 00:15:59.610
See you later.

415
00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:01.890
And put a shot of the reels under Mifflin exterior.

416
00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:03.490
Jenna's getting sassy on you.

417
00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:04.450
Yeah, I'm going to call someone.

418
00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:05.990
Someone get it right.

419
00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:06.850
All right.

420
00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:09.830
Here is my last fast fact for this episode.

421
00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:10.060
Okay, lady.

422
00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:10.150
Okay.

423
00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:12.710
Meredith is not Meredith.

424
00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:13.490
She is not.

425
00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:14.430
She is Henriette.

426
00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:15.370
It's Henriette.

427
00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:16.010
Yes.

428
00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:21.910
Meredith is played by Henriette Mantell in the pilot episode, not Kate Flannery.

429
00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:23.070
This is true.

430
00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:28.890
Henriette Mantell was a comedy actress who had just broken out because she had played Alice

431
00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:30.270
in the Brady Bunch filMs.

432
00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:32.930
And she was there for the pilot.

433
00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:37.110
And then it was six months later that we got picked up.

434
00:16:37.150 --> 00:16:39.770
And then we started filming the rest of the episodes.

435
00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:42.470
And I guess the rumor was she was not available.

436
00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:43.010
She was busy.

437
00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:44.150
And we had to recast her.

438
00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:45.730
And so Kate Flannery came in.

439
00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:47.030
Here is my fast fact.

440
00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:50.630
Kate Flannery and I met years before the office.

441
00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:54.990
We were in a all-female improv group called Bitch Planet.

442
00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:56.110
Woo-hoo!

443
00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:57.070
Aw, Bitch Planet.

444
00:16:57.070 --> 00:16:59.110
Sunday nights at like 10 p.m.

445
00:16:59.170 --> 00:16:59.870
That's when you want to-

446
00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:01.290
It's exactly when you want your comedy.

447
00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:02.250
That's when you want to watch improv.

448
00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:04.350
Kate and I had known each other for a really long time.

449
00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:09.350
And also, Oscar Nunez and I did a sketch comedy show together called Hot Towel.

450
00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:11.130
Where do you get these names, Angela?

451
00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:13.430
So it was really fun for me because here I am.

452
00:17:13.570 --> 00:17:15.490
Like, I was so nervous the first day.

453
00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:17.410
I didn't know Oscar had been caSt.

454
00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:18.790
He didn't know I had been caSt.

455
00:17:19.070 --> 00:17:20.510
You guys, this was sort of before-

456
00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:21.690
This was before smartphones.

457
00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:26.450
It's not like you texted someone you got a role or you put it on your social media.

458
00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:27.810
There was none of that.

459
00:17:27.950 --> 00:17:30.150
So you walked in and were completely surprised.

460
00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:32.190
I was completely surprised when I saw Oscar.

461
00:17:32.530 --> 00:17:37.350
And not only did I see Oscar, but Ken Coapa sat us next to each other.

462
00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:39.410
So I was like, oh my God, I'm going to be okay.

463
00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:40.430
That's amazing.

464
00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:41.390
My buddy's here.

465
00:17:41.630 --> 00:17:44.110
And then Brian walked in as Kevin and I was like, here we go.

466
00:17:44.250 --> 00:17:44.970
We're off to the races.

467
00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:46.810
You guys were amazing in accounting.

468
00:17:47.190 --> 00:17:47.810
Thank you.

469
00:17:56.450 --> 00:17:57.610
I was sitting in my little corner back there.

470
00:17:58.350 --> 00:18:00.590
I remember meeting everyone for the first time.

471
00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:02.370
Do you remember your first impressions of people?

472
00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:03.290
I do.

473
00:18:03.290 --> 00:18:05.050
I remember my first impression of Rainn Wilson.

474
00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:05.870
Tell me.

475
00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:09.970
Well, I met Rainn Wilson at a live test audition.

476
00:18:10.130 --> 00:18:15.210
So for all the leads of the show, they brought us into screen test and they mixed and matched us.

477
00:18:15.290 --> 00:18:18.650
There were four Pams and four Dwights and four Jims and four Michaels.

478
00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:19.010
Yeah.

479
00:18:19.010 --> 00:18:21.890
And we spent an entire day being mixed and matched with each other.

480
00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:30.230
And I remember I met Rainn Wilson and he was in character, but I couldn't really know that because I didn't know Rainn yet.

481
00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:35.090
And I thought he was the weirdest person I'd ever met in my life.

482
00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:36.710
Wait, he was in character the whole time?

483
00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:37.090
Yeah.

484
00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:38.370
He just was white.

485
00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:39.130
Oh, so method, Rainn.

486
00:18:39.130 --> 00:18:40.650
He had his hair like that.

487
00:18:41.050 --> 00:18:41.910
So method.

488
00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:42.630
But you know what?

489
00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:44.330
I kind of did the same thing.

490
00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:47.090
I did my Pam hairstyle.

491
00:18:47.090 --> 00:18:52.030
The hairstyle that I wore on the show, I sort of invented through the audition process.

492
00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:56.590
That sort of half up, half down, scrunchy, air-dried curl.

493
00:18:56.810 --> 00:18:58.970
And so I was very quiet.

494
00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:06.010
I didn't talk to people in the waiting room because I just thought Pam was sort of not a chatter and I didn't want to get out of character.

495
00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:07.830
I remember one of the first things.

496
00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:10.950
This is so random, but it's true.

497
00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:15.170
That first week of filming, Rainn's wife came to have lunch.

498
00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:21.170
And this is the main interaction I remember from Rainn during the pilot episode.

499
00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:23.870
Rainn introduced me to his wife.

500
00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:26.170
She was pregnant at the time with their son.

501
00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:26.610
Yes.

502
00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:30.270
And he said, this is my wife, Holly, bearer of my seed.

503
00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:36.080
And she started laughing.

504
00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:38.360
But that was just Rainn being Rainn.

505
00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:38.840
That's Rainn.

506
00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:39.520
That is Rainn.

507
00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:40.780
But wouldn't Dwight say that?

508
00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:42.060
He would also say that.

509
00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:43.260
This is Angela, bearer of my seed.

510
00:19:43.420 --> 00:19:43.780
Yes.

511
00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:44.400
It's amazing how...

512
00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:45.960
She's carrying my seed.

513
00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:47.260
I'm like, oh my God.

514
00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:50.980
I feel like we all had little parts of our characters in us.

515
00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:54.700
But I don't know if that's true of you because you are so opposite from Angela Martin.

516
00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:55.220
I am.

517
00:19:55.300 --> 00:20:01.340
I was thinking about how you were saying, like, you stayed in character and so did Rainn in between your auditions in the lobby.

518
00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:02.500
I would not have.

519
00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:04.500
I would have been like, chitty chat chat.

520
00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:05.380
Chitty chat chat.

521
00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:08.680
And then I would have just turned into a bitch when I entered the room.

522
00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:13.860
Well, you know, I didn't have any scenes with Rainn in the pilot of Substance.

523
00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:15.080
I didn't have any one-on-one scenes.

524
00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:22.040
So for me, when we were shooting the pilot, the people that I really bonded with were Steve, John, and you.

525
00:20:22.400 --> 00:20:24.240
Because you were over the little part.

526
00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:26.740
You were my closest seatmate in the office.

527
00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:28.140
So you and I bonded.

528
00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:34.600
And I remember after we finished the pilot and we all left and we didn't know if we were going to be picked up or make any more.

529
00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:37.420
Shortly after that, I turned 30 years old.

530
00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:39.580
It was a big milestone birthday for me.

531
00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:43.460
And I really wondered if I should invite any of you guys.

532
00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:44.960
And I didn't.

533
00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:49.580
I didn't invite you guys because I thought, well, I'm probably never going to see them again.

534
00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:53.300
Should I throw you another 30th birthday party and invite everyone?

535
00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:54.500
Would you?

536
00:20:54.740 --> 00:20:56.920
And then we could just say happy 30th.

537
00:20:57.120 --> 00:20:57.840
Will you?

538
00:20:58.420 --> 00:20:58.900
Okay.

539
00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:04.580
Because now you guys are some of my closest friends and you aren't at my milestone birthday.

540
00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:07.760
Okay, we'll have to throw you a 30th birthday party.

541
00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:08.660
It won't be weird.

542
00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:09.840
All right, guys.

543
00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:13.320
So next up, we are going to break down this episode.

544
00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:14.980
We're going to really get into the nitty-gritty.

545
00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:17.420
We're going to talk about scenes and memories and stories.

546
00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:18.240
So stick with us.

547
00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:27.320
We're talking about the pilot episode of The Office.

548
00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:30.340
And I think we should talk about some of our favorite moments, Jenna.

549
00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:30.920
Yes.

550
00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:35.160
Well, one of my favorite moments is the dynamic between Pam and Michael.

551
00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:37.420
I mean, selfishly, I guess I'm in that moment.

552
00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:40.360
But I really loved working with Steve Carell.

553
00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:45.140
I really loved how much he let me be silent and just look at him.

554
00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:45.980
And just react.

555
00:21:46.180 --> 00:21:46.460
Yes.

556
00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:47.340
I loved that.

557
00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:53.380
But I especially love whenever Melora Hardin, who plays Jan Levinson Gould, got in the mix.

558
00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:54.680
She's so commanding.

559
00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:57.900
Just like Melora's presence is like, I'm scared of her.

560
00:21:58.000 --> 00:21:58.340
Yes.

561
00:21:58.520 --> 00:21:59.000
I get it.

562
00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:01.000
I get why he was a little scared of her.

563
00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:08.200
And I love the scene where Michael tries to throw Pam under the bus about why he doesn't have the agenda that he's supposed to have.

564
00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:14.580
And it's because he told her to throw it in the receptacle, the trash can that is a special filing cabinet for corporate memos.

565
00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:17.260
And I remember shooting that scene.

566
00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:26.480
And I just remember every time that Pam said, you told me to put it in a special filing cabinet that is a trash can, that we would giggle.

567
00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:33.440
And so I just remember the matter-of-factness of that moment and how fun that was.

568
00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:36.540
Pam totally stood up for herself in her own little way in that moment.

569
00:22:36.760 --> 00:22:38.140
Here's the thing about Steve Carell.

570
00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:40.900
He is one of the nicest people you will ever meet.

571
00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:42.280
He is so nice.

572
00:22:42.280 --> 00:22:46.020
I know it's almost even become like a joke, like Steve Carell's the nicest person ever.

573
00:22:46.160 --> 00:22:47.140
But he is.

574
00:22:47.320 --> 00:22:47.580
I know.

575
00:22:47.660 --> 00:22:50.220
And people ask me a lot, what's it like to work with Steve Carell?

576
00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:51.940
Are you just laughing all the time?

577
00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:56.320
And no, you're not laughing all the time because Steve isn't on all the time.

578
00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:57.340
He's not one of those—

579
00:22:57.340 --> 00:22:57.760
He's a real person.

580
00:22:57.760 --> 00:22:58.620
He's a real person.

581
00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:02.280
And he's—I've worked with comedians who are on all the time.

582
00:23:02.340 --> 00:23:02.480
Yeah.

583
00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:06.500
Who walk around and you can just—they can't turn off their joke-making brain.

584
00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:07.400
I drive my cursor.

585
00:23:07.640 --> 00:23:08.660
It's a little insane.

586
00:23:09.220 --> 00:23:10.240
Steve is not like that.

587
00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:16.880
But you and Steve and I would sit and have long conversations about the best frozen yogurt in the San Fernando Valley.

588
00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:24.340
Oh, I will never forget the morning—on Monday mornings, Steve would come out of his office, out of Michael Scott's office between scenes.

589
00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:27.640
He would walk over to Jenna and I, and we would talk about our weekends.

590
00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:32.080
And one Monday morning, he walked over, and he was like, ladies, guess what I did this weekend?

591
00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:32.560
We're like, what?

592
00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:33.800
And he goes, I went to Target.

593
00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:35.860
And he would talk to us about Target.

594
00:23:35.860 --> 00:23:37.460
And he told us about his Target run.

595
00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:39.520
It was really, really sweet.

596
00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:48.060
You know, recently, I was in a Michaels purchasing some supplies for a crafting job, and the checkout guy said,

597
00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:51.360
oh, my gosh, now this is getting crazy.

598
00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:54.160
This morning, Oscar Nunez came in here.

599
00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:56.400
Then Steve Carell, and now you?

600
00:23:56.580 --> 00:23:57.800
What, do you guys all live here?

601
00:23:57.960 --> 00:23:59.600
We love crafting.

602
00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:01.080
We love crafting.

603
00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:02.360
And we're all here.

604
00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:06.580
We're all sort of—I guess we all live around this one Michaels crafting store.

605
00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:06.820
Yeah.

606
00:24:06.940 --> 00:24:13.200
I texted Steve, and I said, Steve, the checkout guy at Michaels told me you were buying some crafting supplies today.

607
00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:15.940
And he said, oh, yes, I needed some glue sticks.

608
00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:17.720
He sort of told me what he needed.

609
00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:19.020
He's like, what were you there for?

610
00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:21.140
But that is Steve.

611
00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:21.980
That is Steve.

612
00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:23.940
That's the kind of stuff we would talk to him about.

613
00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:31.240
And so to then—to have that man then shift into this scene where he's just being awful to my character.

614
00:24:31.500 --> 00:24:34.580
Like throwing you under the bus in front of the big boss lady.

615
00:24:34.600 --> 00:24:38.060
Yes, when our real dynamic was just like so much—

616
00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:38.640
So, so different.

617
00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:39.280
So much love.

618
00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:43.600
So in the scene, Jan tells Michael there's going to be downsizing.

619
00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:43.800
Yeah.

620
00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:44.980
This is big news.

621
00:24:45.080 --> 00:24:45.520
Big news.

622
00:24:45.640 --> 00:24:46.100
This is important.

623
00:24:46.660 --> 00:24:48.200
Michael should be paying attention.

624
00:24:48.240 --> 00:24:51.300
We've already established that he's thrown away the agenda for the meeting.

625
00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:52.720
Yeah, he should take this seriously.

626
00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:56.220
But instead, he takes a phone call from his friend, Todd Packer.

627
00:24:56.340 --> 00:25:00.220
Todd Packer, his party buddy, who he clearly covets.

628
00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:01.580
He thinks this guy's amazing.

629
00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:08.500
Another interesting fact about this scene is that the voice of Todd Packer that comes on the phone

630
00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:13.380
is not the voice of the real Todd Packer.

631
00:25:13.540 --> 00:25:15.160
What are you talking about?

632
00:25:15.340 --> 00:25:18.420
Dave Koechner ends up playing Todd Packer.

633
00:25:18.420 --> 00:25:23.020
When we meet him in season two, this role is played by Dave Koechner.

634
00:25:23.540 --> 00:25:26.680
But Dave was not hired when we shot the pilot.

635
00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:27.000
What?

636
00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:27.640
He's not—

637
00:25:27.640 --> 00:25:27.900
Wait, what?

638
00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:28.920
So I was thinking—

639
00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:30.400
It sounds like Dave Koechner.

640
00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:30.980
Who is it?

641
00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:33.200
But I'm thinking it can't be Dave Koechner, right?

642
00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:33.580
It's—

643
00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:35.060
All this time, I thought it was Dave Koechner.

644
00:25:35.060 --> 00:25:35.980
It's not Dave.

645
00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:36.500
Who is it?

646
00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:41.340
I texted Dave Rogers, our editor, knower of all things.

647
00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:43.500
I mean, he is knower of all office facts.

648
00:25:43.520 --> 00:25:43.800
He is.

649
00:25:43.980 --> 00:25:45.080
Okay, what did Dave say?

650
00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:49.720
Dave said that the voice is played by Toby Huss.

651
00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:50.860
Toby Huss!

652
00:25:50.920 --> 00:25:52.780
Yes, very famous voice actor.

653
00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:54.380
He was on King of the Hill for years.

654
00:25:54.580 --> 00:25:55.820
And friends of Greg Daniels.

655
00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:56.040
Yes.

656
00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:57.660
Toby Huss is so talented.

657
00:25:57.800 --> 00:25:58.100
I know.

658
00:25:58.200 --> 00:25:58.900
How about that?

659
00:25:58.940 --> 00:26:01.060
That is Todd Packer's voice.

660
00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:02.360
In the pilot episode.

661
00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:02.740
Yes.

662
00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:07.340
Well, I loved—here's one of the things I loved about the pilot, and just sort of the

663
00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:11.000
brilliance that was Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant and Greg Daniels and their collaboration

664
00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:17.440
building this world out, is that so many, like, truths about the characters were established

665
00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:18.900
in all these subtle ways.

666
00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:24.960
And one of the things I loved is that Michael is clearly thrilled that the camera people are

667
00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:25.240
there.

668
00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:25.720
Oh.

669
00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:26.700
He is so excited.

670
00:26:26.820 --> 00:26:28.580
He could not have signed up for this faster.

671
00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:30.760
I mean, he jumped at the chance at this.

672
00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:33.920
And he thinks he is one of the world's best comedians.

673
00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:34.400
Oh, yeah.

674
00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:34.900
In the world.

675
00:26:35.020 --> 00:26:35.860
Well, he has that—

676
00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:36.920
He is one of the funniest people in the world.

677
00:26:37.060 --> 00:26:41.000
He has that amazing talking head where he talks about who his heroes are.

678
00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:41.440
Yes.

679
00:26:41.680 --> 00:26:43.420
And they are Bob Hope.

680
00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:44.660
Abraham Lincoln.

681
00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:45.680
Bono.

682
00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:46.480
And God.

683
00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:47.100
Probably.

684
00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:48.220
He said probably.

685
00:26:48.220 --> 00:26:48.960
Probably God.

686
00:26:49.220 --> 00:26:50.840
And here's a little tidbit.

687
00:26:51.240 --> 00:26:52.000
Steve wrote that.

688
00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:52.700
He did?

689
00:26:52.900 --> 00:26:53.120
Yes.

690
00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:53.780
Oh, my God.

691
00:26:53.780 --> 00:26:54.300
I love that.

692
00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:55.060
I didn't know that.

693
00:26:55.060 --> 00:27:01.840
Yeah, Greg asked him, would you mind writing a list of who you believe are Michael Scott's

694
00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:02.220
heroes?

695
00:27:02.340 --> 00:27:03.280
Oh, so brilliant.

696
00:27:03.440 --> 00:27:03.540
Bob Hope.

697
00:27:03.540 --> 00:27:04.360
And it made it in.

698
00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:04.860
Yes.

699
00:27:05.080 --> 00:27:09.280
Well, and he just—every chance he gets, he's like looking to the camera and doing his comedy

700
00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:09.700
bits.

701
00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:14.900
And this sort of sets up just Michael's love of being liked and being appreciated.

702
00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:18.560
And he wants everyone to think he's like the fun boss.

703
00:27:18.560 --> 00:27:20.820
And he thinks he's legitimately talented.

704
00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:24.500
Well, there's that scene where he does the $6 million man where he's walking away from

705
00:27:24.500 --> 00:27:25.080
Pam's desk.

706
00:27:25.220 --> 00:27:25.520
Yes.

707
00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:28.400
I could not get through that scene.

708
00:27:28.540 --> 00:27:33.380
And in fact, in order to get my reaction shot, they had to make Steve leave the set

709
00:27:34.380 --> 00:27:36.260
because I couldn't get through it.

710
00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:42.900
So that reaction of me was not while Steve was actually doing anything because every time

711
00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:44.300
he did it, I lost it.

712
00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:44.740
Yeah.

713
00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:47.600
Well, he's so giddy to do his $6 million man.

714
00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:53.100
I timed it because as I watched it, I was like, wow, this is a long time to commit to

715
00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:56.160
this bit because he's like, fa-da-da-da-da-da-da-da, ba-da-da-da-da-da.

716
00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:57.160
He did it.

717
00:27:57.320 --> 00:28:02.520
It started at 9 minutes, 40 seconds, and it doesn't end until 10 minutes, 2 seconds.

718
00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:03.260
Oh, my gosh.

719
00:28:03.260 --> 00:28:06.760
That is 22 seconds of the slow-mo bit.

720
00:28:07.160 --> 00:28:08.360
I think that's why—

721
00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:09.180
That's a ton.

722
00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:15.020
When you think of like the whole scope of an episode for 22 seconds, he went, fa-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da.

723
00:28:15.500 --> 00:28:17.900
I mean, that's probably why I couldn't get through it.

724
00:28:17.980 --> 00:28:21.460
That's why they couldn't get a reaction of me because I couldn't hold it for 20 seconds.

725
00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:23.180
I couldn't hold a straight face that long.

726
00:28:23.260 --> 00:28:27.060
And he was so desperate for you to look at him and have some kind of reaction.

727
00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:28.280
Oh, man.

728
00:28:28.500 --> 00:28:28.700
Okay.

729
00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:29.320
What else?

730
00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:29.720
What else?

731
00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:31.540
There's the conference room scene.

732
00:28:31.680 --> 00:28:32.340
Oh, yes.

733
00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:37.500
Where Michael officially announces that there is going to be downsizing at Dunder Mifflin.

734
00:28:45.020 --> 00:28:54.000
They're going to no longer support both a Scranton branch and a Stanford branch and that they're going to have to prove who is the branch that should stay.

735
00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:59.220
But even if Scranton gets to stay, there might be downsizing of employees.

736
00:28:59.460 --> 00:29:03.280
Yeah, the company's hurting, and they have to make some cutbacks, and they have to fire some people.

737
00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:09.500
Yes, and so Michael goes into the conference room, and this is the first time we see Michael in the conference room.

738
00:29:09.620 --> 00:29:12.460
And, of course, Dwight won't give him the space to do that.

739
00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:15.500
It's their first power struggle, which you'll see for years to come.

740
00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:20.800
It's the first time he refers to himself as the assistant regional manager, which is great.

741
00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:23.560
And I noticed something about this conference room scene.

742
00:29:23.720 --> 00:29:24.060
What?

743
00:29:24.300 --> 00:29:25.900
I bet I have it on my note card.

744
00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:32.480
I bet you have it, that there are some extras in this scene that are never to be seen again.

745
00:29:32.740 --> 00:29:33.600
There are two women.

746
00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:35.180
Do you want me to refer to it?

747
00:29:35.320 --> 00:29:37.140
Let me just tell you what I wrote on my note card.

748
00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:37.420
Okay.

749
00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:39.880
Conference room faces, semicolon.

750
00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:49.660
So, in the background, there are two women, and they are the real production company accountants for our show.

751
00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:55.920
Yes, we needed more people to fill out the space, and they pulled in our actual accountants from our production office.

752
00:29:56.060 --> 00:30:01.080
Yes, Ken Guapas, the director, walked into the production office and asked if anyone would like to be in the background of a scene.

753
00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:04.280
And those two women came and sat there for that couple of hours.

754
00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:05.560
They were so giddy.

755
00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:06.720
They loved it.

756
00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:07.360
They loved it.

757
00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:07.840
They loved it.

758
00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:15.320
And then also, in the conference room, some faces you might see sprinkled through the first season, but then they don't go past that season, are Luann Kelly,

759
00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:17.080
who worked in the Annex.

760
00:30:17.260 --> 00:30:17.460
Yes.

761
00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:18.640
She is someone that you see a lot.

762
00:30:18.780 --> 00:30:21.420
I get asked a lot, who is the lady in the Annex?

763
00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:26.580
Yes, she was sort of over where Mindy ends up sitting, by Kelly's desk.

764
00:30:26.720 --> 00:30:32.440
And then also, of course, Devin and Creed, who ultimately do have a storyline that will come later.

765
00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:33.040
Season two.

766
00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:33.700
That's right.

767
00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:38.320
So for an entire season, you just see Devin and Creed in the background of scenes.

768
00:30:38.480 --> 00:30:40.360
They don't get any lines until the second season.

769
00:30:40.360 --> 00:30:44.240
But that is the only scene our actual accountants are in, because then they had to go back to work.

770
00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:44.720
They did.

771
00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:47.220
They had to actually go back to being accountants for the show.

772
00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:49.020
I think this is an interesting tidbit.

773
00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:56.060
Phyllis Smith, who plays Phyllis on the show, she was the casting associate for The Office.

774
00:30:56.060 --> 00:31:01.180
So when I auditioned for The Office, she read my audition scenes with me.

775
00:31:01.580 --> 00:31:01.920
Everyone.

776
00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:03.760
I read my scene with Phyllis.

777
00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:05.100
That's who I read my scene with.

778
00:31:05.200 --> 00:31:05.520
Yes.

779
00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:10.340
And Ken Kwap, as the director, was so taken with how she did her readings with the actors.

780
00:31:10.340 --> 00:31:11.720
She's so earnest, you guys.

781
00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:13.020
She is so earneSt.

782
00:31:13.300 --> 00:31:16.660
And so real that he said to Greg Daniels, I want her on the show.

783
00:31:17.220 --> 00:31:18.960
Can I give her a part on the show?

784
00:31:19.280 --> 00:31:25.700
And so Phyllis went from being a casting associate, which she had done for years with Alison Jones,

785
00:31:25.700 --> 00:31:30.760
the casting director, worked with Phyllis, to having a part on this pilot.

786
00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:35.520
And I think because we all thought the show was going nowhere, it was fine.

787
00:31:35.580 --> 00:31:38.140
And after the pilot was over, she went back to being a casting associate.

788
00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:39.180
She went right back to work.

789
00:31:39.640 --> 00:31:44.620
Phyllis has a great story that she tells that the way she found out that she got Phyllis

790
00:31:44.620 --> 00:31:47.400
is that she got sides and they said, Phyllis.

791
00:31:48.320 --> 00:31:51.620
Oh, so there was a character and they named it Phyllis.

792
00:31:51.640 --> 00:31:53.260
And Ken was like, that's you.

793
00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:54.000
You're Phyllis.

794
00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:54.920
And she was like, what?

795
00:31:55.780 --> 00:31:59.640
We wrote a part for you based on the numerous readings with actors.

796
00:31:59.860 --> 00:32:01.240
You're now in the show, Phyllis.

797
00:32:01.360 --> 00:32:06.020
Which is just so cool because Phyllis had wanted to be an actress.

798
00:32:06.300 --> 00:32:07.100
She had been an actress.

799
00:32:07.240 --> 00:32:08.100
She had been a burlesque dancer.

800
00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:08.820
Burlesque dancer.

801
00:32:09.100 --> 00:32:10.080
No one can shimmy.

802
00:32:10.240 --> 00:32:11.660
No one can shimmy like Phyllis.

803
00:32:11.860 --> 00:32:12.200
That's right.

804
00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:13.280
She has a great shimmy.

805
00:32:13.440 --> 00:32:18.640
She had an amazing photo on her desk because Ken Quapis wanted us to personalize our desk

806
00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:20.480
for the pilot episode and moving forward.

807
00:32:20.660 --> 00:32:22.460
So we all brought in something from home.

808
00:32:22.460 --> 00:32:28.000
I have a photo of myself and my grandmother in a frame that says, I heart grandma.

809
00:32:28.380 --> 00:32:32.380
And my eyes are closed in the photo, but my grandmother framed it anyway.

810
00:32:33.300 --> 00:32:37.820
And so I have that on my desk and it just kind of like tickled me and made me think of

811
00:32:37.820 --> 00:32:38.320
my grandmother.

812
00:32:38.640 --> 00:32:41.300
I had a picture of me and my sister swimming in Lake of the Ozarks.

813
00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:41.780
Yes.

814
00:32:41.920 --> 00:32:48.680
And Phyllis had a picture of herself in her full burlesque outfit with a red feather boa draped

815
00:32:48.680 --> 00:32:49.520
over her shoulders.

816
00:32:50.080 --> 00:32:51.240
Angela, let's take a quick break.

817
00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:52.040
Okay, Jenna.

818
00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:53.620
You're going to get a snack.

819
00:32:53.920 --> 00:32:54.400
Why?

820
00:32:55.060 --> 00:32:57.260
Because you always get a snack during the breaks.

821
00:32:57.420 --> 00:32:57.780
I do.

822
00:33:03.200 --> 00:33:08.520
So then we get to that crazy scene where Michael is walking Ryan the temp around.

823
00:33:08.520 --> 00:33:11.880
So he's just been told that we need downsizing.

824
00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:13.600
And then he hires a temp.

825
00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:17.420
It's the opposite of downsizing.

826
00:33:17.480 --> 00:33:18.140
It's the opposite.

827
00:33:18.420 --> 00:33:22.380
Can I just say one thing I love too is when Dwight has his talking head and he said,

828
00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:24.180
I've been talking about downsizing for years.

829
00:33:24.180 --> 00:33:25.400
I brought it up in my interview.

830
00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:27.660
Like, who does that?

831
00:33:27.760 --> 00:33:31.060
He was like interviewing for a job and is like, you should downsize.

832
00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:33.080
I would like this job.

833
00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:35.100
I suggest you get rid of three other people.

834
00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:37.560
So here's a little fun fact.

835
00:33:37.560 --> 00:33:38.980
It's not a fast fact.

836
00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:43.140
But a fun fact is that BJ Novak was the first person cast on the show.

837
00:33:43.700 --> 00:33:44.560
He was?

838
00:33:44.740 --> 00:33:45.140
He was.

839
00:33:45.400 --> 00:33:50.260
Greg Daniels saw him doing stand-up comedy and he thought he was like, this guy.

840
00:33:50.480 --> 00:33:51.720
I want this guy.

841
00:33:51.880 --> 00:33:59.040
And so he offered BJ a role as both a writer and the role of Ryan the temp on the show.

842
00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:03.200
So when I came in for my audition, BJ was already caSt.

843
00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:04.640
He was the very first person.

844
00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:10.080
And he was also the first example of that synergy of having people who were both performers

845
00:34:10.080 --> 00:34:11.360
and writers for the show.

846
00:34:11.440 --> 00:34:12.660
Which was so great.

847
00:34:12.880 --> 00:34:16.600
Something that we haven't talked about, but we will talk about it many times as the show

848
00:34:16.600 --> 00:34:21.660
goes, is that what a wonderful creative collaboration we had between the writers and the actors.

849
00:34:21.860 --> 00:34:23.300
And that was because of Greg Daniels.

850
00:34:23.300 --> 00:34:26.740
He sort of set the tone that he wanted this creative collaboration.

851
00:34:26.740 --> 00:34:30.000
He wanted to hear our ideas and he wanted writers on the set.

852
00:34:30.140 --> 00:34:33.220
And I think we were just so much the better for it.

853
00:34:33.420 --> 00:34:37.280
I've been on other shows now where the writers and the actors are separate and there's not

854
00:34:37.280 --> 00:34:38.080
a lot of collaboration.

855
00:34:38.660 --> 00:34:42.120
It feels weird after having experienced something else.

856
00:34:42.440 --> 00:34:45.880
No, Greg really trusted us to be the experts of our characters.

857
00:34:46.160 --> 00:34:52.020
So whenever he was trying to mull something over or justify something, he would come to us

858
00:34:52.020 --> 00:34:56.360
and ask us, just in the way that he went to Steve and said, hey, do you have any thoughts

859
00:34:56.360 --> 00:34:58.480
on who Michael Scott's heroes would be?

860
00:34:58.660 --> 00:34:58.840
Yeah.

861
00:34:58.920 --> 00:35:03.300
He trusted us to have done our homework and know our characters so well that we could contribute.

862
00:35:03.640 --> 00:35:04.400
Yeah, it was great.

863
00:35:04.460 --> 00:35:05.200
It was really special.

864
00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:09.940
So also in that scene, that is the famous stapler in Jell-O.

865
00:35:10.340 --> 00:35:10.580
Yeah.

866
00:35:10.900 --> 00:35:11.880
The stapler goes in Jell-O.

867
00:35:12.180 --> 00:35:14.480
I have seen so many people recreate this.

868
00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:15.380
How?

869
00:35:15.540 --> 00:35:15.820
How?

870
00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:16.920
I am blown away.

871
00:35:17.080 --> 00:35:21.680
Every time I see a fan tag me in a photo where they put something in Jell-O, I'm like,

872
00:35:21.680 --> 00:35:22.620
I'm very impressed.

873
00:35:22.720 --> 00:35:23.400
Way to go.

874
00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:24.000
I know.

875
00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:28.180
Something we haven't mentioned is the flirtation between Jim and Pam in this episode.

876
00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:35.180
And it goes on through the whole first two-thirds of this episode or three-quarters of this

877
00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:35.640
episode.

878
00:35:35.980 --> 00:35:37.320
Little, little things.

879
00:35:37.720 --> 00:35:40.100
What did I write on my pink note card, Jenna?

880
00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:41.220
Mixed berries.

881
00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:42.120
In quotes.

882
00:35:42.580 --> 00:35:43.520
He's on to me.

883
00:35:43.780 --> 00:35:43.960
Yeah.

884
00:35:44.420 --> 00:35:45.780
That is one of the cutest things.

885
00:35:45.940 --> 00:35:47.100
It is so pure.

886
00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:47.700
It is.

887
00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:51.620
It's like, that's flirting for them, that he knows your favorite yogurt.

888
00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:58.100
But up until the end, you are just sort of following this flirtation between the two

889
00:35:58.100 --> 00:35:58.540
characters.

890
00:35:58.540 --> 00:36:01.880
And then all of a sudden, Roy walks in the door.

891
00:36:02.200 --> 00:36:03.160
David Dinman.

892
00:36:03.320 --> 00:36:04.800
Carrying that weird trash bag.

893
00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:06.520
We never know what's in that trash bag.

894
00:36:06.700 --> 00:36:07.500
What is he carrying?

895
00:36:07.500 --> 00:36:11.380
I loved it so much when John in his gym is like, what's in the bag?

896
00:36:11.560 --> 00:36:12.780
And Roy's like, yeah, just tell her.

897
00:36:12.780 --> 00:36:14.320
Yeah, tell her I'll meet her in the car.

898
00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:14.580
Yeah.

899
00:36:15.060 --> 00:36:22.180
But I think it is such a, I remember watching the pilot with people and having them be shocked

900
00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:23.920
by like, oh, wait.

901
00:36:24.020 --> 00:36:24.420
Oh, crap.

902
00:36:24.420 --> 00:36:26.140
She's been engaged three years?

903
00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:26.980
Three years to this guy.

904
00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:27.620
Holy moly.

905
00:36:27.920 --> 00:36:28.180
Yeah.

906
00:36:28.420 --> 00:36:30.480
And then Jim has that heartbreaking talking head.

907
00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:32.420
Do I think I'll be invited to the wedding?

908
00:36:33.280 --> 00:36:34.380
Oh, Jim.

909
00:36:35.100 --> 00:36:35.980
Breaking hearts.

910
00:36:36.200 --> 00:36:37.220
John Krasinski, breaking hearts.

911
00:36:37.220 --> 00:36:38.360
He's breaking hearts right out of the gate.

912
00:36:38.360 --> 00:36:39.080
He's breaking hearts.

913
00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:39.860
He really is.

914
00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:44.460
So another really cute flirty moment between Jim and Pam, after the conference room scene,

915
00:36:44.620 --> 00:36:49.000
where we all find out there's going to be downsizing, the camera cuts to different

916
00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:51.080
groups of people talking about downsizing.

917
00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:55.140
But when it cuts to Jim and Pam, we are not talking about downsizing.

918
00:36:55.140 --> 00:36:56.220
What are you guys talking about?

919
00:36:56.780 --> 00:36:59.460
We are talking about your cat party.

920
00:36:59.660 --> 00:37:00.760
Do you remember this?

921
00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:01.360
I do.

922
00:37:01.520 --> 00:37:02.060
I do.

923
00:37:02.320 --> 00:37:06.780
So this is, I feel like this is really significant and kind of goes on the heels of what we were

924
00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:09.360
talking about with Greg wanting to collaborate with actors.

925
00:37:09.860 --> 00:37:13.440
So Ken said, listen, I'm just going to roll the camera.

926
00:37:13.740 --> 00:37:15.260
You two just talk to each other.

927
00:37:15.780 --> 00:37:16.800
Don't push it.

928
00:37:17.080 --> 00:37:17.840
Just talk.

929
00:37:18.060 --> 00:37:19.260
Make each other laugh.

930
00:37:19.500 --> 00:37:23.920
And so here's something you should know is that earlier in the scene, Ken had asked

931
00:37:23.920 --> 00:37:26.180
me if I would pass out papers in the background.

932
00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:26.660
Yeah.

933
00:37:26.920 --> 00:37:28.580
I only had three lines in the episode.

934
00:37:28.900 --> 00:37:33.380
They were, you know, otherwise I was just background basically for that, you know, pilot.

935
00:37:33.800 --> 00:37:39.760
So he asked me if I would pass out papers and I had just doodled a little drawing of a cat

936
00:37:39.760 --> 00:37:42.440
and I said, you're invited to Sprinkle's birthday party.

937
00:37:42.640 --> 00:37:46.120
I just sort of made up that my character had found a cat in the parking lot.

938
00:37:46.260 --> 00:37:46.500
I don't know.

939
00:37:46.520 --> 00:37:47.360
I was just sort of bored.

940
00:37:47.540 --> 00:37:49.120
And we all made up backstories.

941
00:37:49.120 --> 00:37:54.040
Yeah, I just made up backstory that my character had found this cat in the parking lot.

942
00:37:54.080 --> 00:37:59.300
But I think it's really significant because you in your character actory prep work decided

943
00:37:59.300 --> 00:38:01.500
that your character was a crazy cat lady.

944
00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:02.580
I guess I did.

945
00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:05.520
Because you kind of decorated your desk with some cat stuff.

946
00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:11.460
Now, the prop department that set my desk had a paper clip holder that was a cat laying

947
00:38:11.460 --> 00:38:13.240
on its side and it holds paper clips.

948
00:38:13.360 --> 00:38:13.580
Okay.

949
00:38:13.580 --> 00:38:14.560
I took it from my desk.

950
00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:15.200
I still have it.

951
00:38:15.760 --> 00:38:18.080
I might have stolen it as a memento.

952
00:38:18.240 --> 00:38:20.860
And so I think I was just looking at that and I was doodling.

953
00:38:21.040 --> 00:38:25.780
And truth be told, there's only about three things I can draw in real life that look like

954
00:38:25.780 --> 00:38:26.660
the actual thing.

955
00:38:26.780 --> 00:38:27.600
And one of them is a cat.

956
00:38:27.640 --> 00:38:28.440
One of them is a cat.

957
00:38:28.620 --> 00:38:29.680
That's kind of true for me too.

958
00:38:29.680 --> 00:38:30.760
I can draw a palm tree.

959
00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:32.000
I can draw a cat.

960
00:38:32.840 --> 00:38:34.040
And I can draw Snoopy.

961
00:38:34.680 --> 00:38:35.040
Hey!

962
00:38:35.180 --> 00:38:35.820
There you go.

963
00:38:36.020 --> 00:38:37.900
And I guess I had just doodled a cat.

964
00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:43.920
And so I'd written on – so maybe my poor skills at drawing is also what gave birth

965
00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:44.520
to this moment.

966
00:38:44.600 --> 00:38:47.560
But I drew this cat and I wrote, you're invited to Sprinkle's birthday party in the

967
00:38:47.560 --> 00:38:48.000
parking lot.

968
00:38:48.040 --> 00:38:48.980
And you gave it to me.

969
00:38:49.040 --> 00:38:50.360
I handed it to you earlier.

970
00:38:50.360 --> 00:38:50.820
I remember.

971
00:38:50.980 --> 00:38:52.200
And it was weird.

972
00:38:52.200 --> 00:38:56.280
I remember getting it in the background of the scene and thinking, I don't know what

973
00:38:56.280 --> 00:38:56.760
this is.

974
00:38:56.940 --> 00:38:58.860
But I stuck it to my computer monitor.

975
00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:01.300
Yeah, it was on a Post-it and you stuck it to your computer.

976
00:39:01.300 --> 00:39:07.240
So, when Ken Kwap has asked me to talk to John about anything, to do this scene, just

977
00:39:07.240 --> 00:39:10.400
talk to each other, I noticed that Post-it note.

978
00:39:10.640 --> 00:39:15.500
And I said, are you going to go – something like, I can't remember, cat party on Sunday?

979
00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:16.360
And he –

980
00:39:16.360 --> 00:39:17.620
He's like, oh no, it's ridiculous.

981
00:39:17.720 --> 00:39:18.840
He's like, no, definitely not.

982
00:39:18.980 --> 00:39:19.800
And then we giggle.

983
00:39:20.180 --> 00:39:23.120
And that is the bit that they left in the episode.

984
00:39:23.320 --> 00:39:24.820
And that's the birth of Sprinkles.

985
00:39:24.940 --> 00:39:26.940
That was the birth of Sprinkles.

986
00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:30.520
That turned into this cat birthday party.

987
00:39:30.520 --> 00:39:31.740
Greg honed in on that.

988
00:39:31.860 --> 00:39:33.380
He was like, what is the cat birthday party?

989
00:39:33.540 --> 00:39:38.820
And I said, well, Angela gave me this Post-it note about her birthday party for her cat

990
00:39:38.820 --> 00:39:39.360
or something.

991
00:39:39.560 --> 00:39:40.680
And he loved it.

992
00:39:40.780 --> 00:39:47.100
And then for four seasons, we discussed Sprinkles until his untimely death in the freezer.

993
00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:47.220
Oh, okay.

994
00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:48.800
Yes, but that's another episode.

995
00:39:48.800 --> 00:39:50.420
I won't make you think about it.

996
00:39:50.500 --> 00:39:52.000
Because it's one of my favorite Michael moments, too.

997
00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:57.760
Yeah, so that was really fun, how just these little elements of us in the background sort

998
00:39:57.760 --> 00:40:00.500
of being bored and improvising made it into the show.

999
00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:00.880
Yeah.

1000
00:40:00.980 --> 00:40:01.920
It's so special to me.

1001
00:40:02.320 --> 00:40:06.460
All right, so I have something that I am so curious about.

1002
00:40:06.620 --> 00:40:08.000
And Jenna, I don't even know if you know this.

1003
00:40:08.140 --> 00:40:13.100
Fans out there, I don't know if you know it or if we need to text Dave Rogers, our editor

1004
00:40:13.100 --> 00:40:13.820
extraordinaire.

1005
00:40:14.200 --> 00:40:19.860
So, my D'Angela fans, did you notice in the pilot episode that Dwight is singing Little

1006
00:40:19.860 --> 00:40:22.760
Drummer Boy as he unpacks his desk?

1007
00:40:23.480 --> 00:40:23.920
Angela.

1008
00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:29.860
He's singing like, come, they told me, but then he goes, do-do-do-do, do-do-do-do.

1009
00:40:30.200 --> 00:40:38.360
And then in season three, I karaoke Little Drummer Boy and Dwight joins in and sings with

1010
00:40:38.360 --> 00:40:38.560
me.

1011
00:40:38.940 --> 00:40:42.480
Now, that story is that I was very nervous to sing.

1012
00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:44.000
I don't like singing in front of people.

1013
00:40:44.300 --> 00:40:48.960
And Rain, as Dwight, just jumped in and kind of like supported me so I wasn't up there alone.

1014
00:40:49.100 --> 00:40:51.680
And he kneeled down and he held the microphone for me.

1015
00:40:51.680 --> 00:40:53.820
And I'm just like, oh, my God.

1016
00:40:54.360 --> 00:40:56.200
I mean, what?

1017
00:40:56.440 --> 00:40:57.220
That's weird.

1018
00:40:57.840 --> 00:41:00.100
I feel like that has to be a coincidence.

1019
00:41:00.460 --> 00:41:01.620
I think it's a coincidence.

1020
00:41:01.620 --> 00:41:08.920
But how—and so many fans say to me, like, what amazing synergy it is that Dwight is singing

1021
00:41:08.920 --> 00:41:10.420
Little Drummer Boy in the pilot.

1022
00:41:10.600 --> 00:41:13.580
And then in season three, I sing it to him.

1023
00:41:13.960 --> 00:41:15.440
Now, did a writer remember that?

1024
00:41:15.560 --> 00:41:19.640
Was that something that like—or was that just in the subconscious of the show?

1025
00:41:19.640 --> 00:41:20.300
I don't know.

1026
00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:20.760
I don't know.

1027
00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:21.860
You know what I found?

1028
00:41:21.960 --> 00:41:25.520
I found this article, and I can't remember where.

1029
00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:26.140
I apologize.

1030
00:41:26.500 --> 00:41:31.580
I found an article that was all about secret messages in the pilot episode.

1031
00:41:32.180 --> 00:41:37.500
And one of the secret messages that someone points out is that in the scene where Roy

1032
00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:41.820
comes in the door for the first time, the boom mic dips into frame.

1033
00:41:43.100 --> 00:41:49.640
And someone wondered, was that a nod to the boom operator, Brian, that we will meet in

1034
00:41:49.640 --> 00:41:50.420
season nine?

1035
00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:52.260
No, that was just an accident.

1036
00:41:52.800 --> 00:42:01.920
Was it foreshadowing to let us know that there are three men in Pam's life, Jim, Roy, and Brian?

1037
00:42:02.340 --> 00:42:02.760
No, no.

1038
00:42:02.800 --> 00:42:06.140
That was just a boom operator accident, and they left it in because it's a documentary.

1039
00:42:06.140 --> 00:42:08.320
Yes, we left that stuff in all the time.

1040
00:42:08.560 --> 00:42:11.580
That was a thing that we coveted that.

1041
00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:12.440
We loved it.

1042
00:42:12.540 --> 00:42:19.160
If the camera person fumbled or if the boom accidentally wasn't shot, we still used it, and we could.

1043
00:42:19.300 --> 00:42:24.900
And that was actually great because sometimes you're on a set and your best take is ruined

1044
00:42:24.900 --> 00:42:31.180
because of some technical accident on the set, but we were able to use all of it.

1045
00:42:31.280 --> 00:42:33.020
We didn't have to put it on the cutting room floor.

1046
00:42:33.280 --> 00:42:34.580
Okay, so yeah, so no conspiracy.

1047
00:42:34.840 --> 00:42:39.240
But yeah, I don't know if Dwight singing Little Drummer Boy in the pilot and Angela singing it

1048
00:42:39.240 --> 00:42:44.920
in Benihana Christmas in season three is just a crazy coincidence, but it's cool, right?

1049
00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:46.120
It's a pretty cool observation.

1050
00:42:46.120 --> 00:42:46.300
It's pretty cool.

1051
00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:51.980
I actually really love these fan theories because I like to think that these accidents

1052
00:42:51.980 --> 00:42:57.300
accidents or coincidences, if they are accidents, are part of a fun, collective, unconscious

1053
00:42:57.300 --> 00:43:00.200
thing that kind of lend itself to the magic of the show.

1054
00:43:01.020 --> 00:43:03.440
Wow, Jenna, that was really deep.

1055
00:43:03.640 --> 00:43:04.380
It kind of was.

1056
00:43:04.440 --> 00:43:06.020
I got real philosophical there.

1057
00:43:06.020 --> 00:43:10.620
Oh my gosh, I feel like I just had a vision of what you were like in college or something.

1058
00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:10.960
Yeah.

1059
00:43:11.120 --> 00:43:16.040
Like you sat around, maybe you wore black turtlenecks and I don't know.

1060
00:43:16.180 --> 00:43:18.800
I was kind of like Art Show Pam.

1061
00:43:19.120 --> 00:43:19.700
Art Show Pam!

1062
00:43:19.700 --> 00:43:21.560
With my turtleneck, my jumper.

1063
00:43:21.560 --> 00:43:22.640
Well, I like it.

1064
00:43:22.760 --> 00:43:23.420
Spilling philosophy.

1065
00:43:23.720 --> 00:43:25.360
All right, Angela, what have you got for us?

1066
00:43:25.480 --> 00:43:28.960
I only had one speaking scene in the pilot and so did Brian.

1067
00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:33.280
It was back in accounting when Angela and Kevin are trying to figure out who's going

1068
00:43:33.280 --> 00:43:34.620
to be fired for downsizing.

1069
00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:36.980
We were really nervous about shooting the scene.

1070
00:43:37.080 --> 00:43:38.380
It's the only time we speak.

1071
00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:43.560
And it was at the end of the day, Ken Kuapas, who's, you know, we've said the nicest

1072
00:43:43.560 --> 00:43:44.980
guy, was like, go ahead.

1073
00:43:45.540 --> 00:43:48.080
And I said, somebody's going to be fired.

1074
00:43:48.120 --> 00:43:49.220
It'll probably be me.

1075
00:43:49.220 --> 00:43:51.740
And Brian is coming and says, yeah, it'll probably be you.

1076
00:43:53.200 --> 00:43:56.380
And Ken came up to me and goes, hey, Angela, can you do it again?

1077
00:43:56.460 --> 00:43:58.560
And like, just give it like half.

1078
00:43:58.980 --> 00:43:59.860
Give it half.

1079
00:44:00.860 --> 00:44:02.540
So I guess I was like, was I too big?

1080
00:44:02.660 --> 00:44:03.820
Was I like, somebody's going to be fired.

1081
00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:04.620
It'll probably be me.

1082
00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:07.740
Like, and then so I was like, somebody will probably be fired.

1083
00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:08.460
It'll probably be me.

1084
00:44:09.040 --> 00:44:09.760
Someone's going to get fired.

1085
00:44:09.900 --> 00:44:10.980
Is that the exact line?

1086
00:44:11.120 --> 00:44:11.980
Somebody's going to be fired.

1087
00:44:12.140 --> 00:44:12.780
It'll probably be me.

1088
00:44:13.160 --> 00:44:15.420
Then he came up and he goes, half of that.

1089
00:44:16.260 --> 00:44:17.140
I said, oh, OK.

1090
00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:19.340
So then I was like, somebody's going to be fired.

1091
00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:20.100
It'll probably be me.

1092
00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:23.280
And he goes, half of that.

1093
00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:23.900
Oh, my God.

1094
00:44:23.900 --> 00:44:24.840
I said, oh, my God.

1095
00:44:25.020 --> 00:44:28.720
I'm going to get fired because I'm like an improv sketch comedy actor.

1096
00:44:28.720 --> 00:44:29.980
And clearly, I'm too broad.

1097
00:44:30.480 --> 00:44:35.980
And so ultimately, the take they use, by the end of it, I literally was like, somebody's

1098
00:44:35.980 --> 00:44:36.820
going to be fired.

1099
00:44:36.860 --> 00:44:37.620
It'll probably be me.

1100
00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:39.920
And then Brian's like, yeah, it'll probably be you.

1101
00:44:40.100 --> 00:44:41.520
And Ken was like, we got it.

1102
00:44:41.700 --> 00:44:42.280
I was like.

1103
00:44:42.500 --> 00:44:45.400
Do you want to point out what you noticed about this scene as well?

1104
00:44:45.460 --> 00:44:46.180
About Kevin?

1105
00:44:46.600 --> 00:44:51.240
Here's my tidbit, guys, is that when I watch this and Brian, I want to call you about

1106
00:44:51.240 --> 00:44:53.580
it, you just sound like Brian to me.

1107
00:44:53.680 --> 00:44:55.240
You don't sound like the Kevin voice.

1108
00:44:55.420 --> 00:44:56.240
OK, can we play it?

1109
00:44:56.500 --> 00:44:58.460
I bet it's going to be me, probably.

1110
00:44:58.720 --> 00:44:59.320
It'll be me.

1111
00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:01.560
Yeah, it'll be you.

1112
00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:02.660
That's Brian!

1113
00:45:02.660 --> 00:45:03.760
Oh, my gosh!

1114
00:45:03.780 --> 00:45:04.560
That's Brian!

1115
00:45:04.960 --> 00:45:06.200
That is Brian's voice.

1116
00:45:06.360 --> 00:45:06.740
That's not.

1117
00:45:06.820 --> 00:45:09.680
Because Kevin would have been like, yeah, it'll probably be me.

1118
00:45:09.680 --> 00:45:10.880
It'll probably be me.

1119
00:45:11.020 --> 00:45:11.220
Yeah.

1120
00:45:11.220 --> 00:45:12.080
That's a horrible Kevin.

1121
00:45:12.080 --> 00:45:14.080
We just really did not do that well.

1122
00:45:14.080 --> 00:45:14.520
Oh, my gosh.

1123
00:45:14.520 --> 00:45:15.860
I want to call Brian about it.

1124
00:45:15.960 --> 00:45:18.220
But when I watched it, I was like, wait a second.

1125
00:45:18.800 --> 00:45:19.900
It's like, that's Brian.

1126
00:45:20.460 --> 00:45:24.820
But I think in the pilot, we were still sort of like figuring out our characters and who

1127
00:45:24.820 --> 00:45:25.320
they were.

1128
00:45:26.240 --> 00:45:28.020
However, I have to say, Angela.

1129
00:45:28.300 --> 00:45:28.480
What?

1130
00:45:28.720 --> 00:45:30.900
I went in to the deleted scenes.

1131
00:45:30.960 --> 00:45:31.780
Oh, my God.

1132
00:45:31.780 --> 00:45:32.620
On the DVD.

1133
00:45:32.920 --> 00:45:33.140
Oh, Lord.

1134
00:45:33.140 --> 00:45:33.940
Season one DVD.

1135
00:45:34.400 --> 00:45:34.660
And?

1136
00:45:34.740 --> 00:45:38.620
And there is, this is something we did on the pilot.

1137
00:45:38.960 --> 00:45:44.860
Some of it was an acting exercise where they called us each into the conference room separately

1138
00:45:44.860 --> 00:45:50.680
and they just asked us unscripted lines about our characters and about our relationship

1139
00:45:50.680 --> 00:45:51.600
to other characters.

1140
00:45:51.760 --> 00:45:52.660
I remember this.

1141
00:45:52.760 --> 00:45:53.140
It wasn't written.

1142
00:45:53.140 --> 00:45:53.300
It wasn't written.

1143
00:45:53.460 --> 00:45:54.020
It was not written.

1144
00:45:54.100 --> 00:45:55.100
We just improvised.

1145
00:45:55.300 --> 00:46:01.160
Oh, so this was sort of, I think, an exercise that Ken wanted to do that Greg and Ken came

1146
00:46:01.160 --> 00:46:01.540
up with.

1147
00:46:01.660 --> 00:46:06.920
In addition to that thing where they filmed us all just working, they also filmed us being

1148
00:46:06.920 --> 00:46:08.140
interviewed in character.

1149
00:46:08.520 --> 00:46:09.940
And I think Greg thought, this will be great.

1150
00:46:09.940 --> 00:46:12.620
Maybe I could use some of these little tidbits here and there.

1151
00:46:12.880 --> 00:46:16.920
Well, there is a series of interviews with each of the members of accounting.

1152
00:46:17.180 --> 00:46:17.760
Oh, my God.

1153
00:46:17.800 --> 00:46:21.840
Where you guys are all trash talking each other very subtly.

1154
00:46:22.580 --> 00:46:30.100
And Angela, you have this incredible deleted scene talking head where they ask you about Oscar.

1155
00:46:30.400 --> 00:46:32.220
Is this where I compare him to a stapler?

1156
00:46:32.680 --> 00:46:33.080
Yes.

1157
00:46:33.360 --> 00:46:35.320
Sam, can you pull that up so we can listen to that?

1158
00:46:35.500 --> 00:46:36.840
Oscar, how do I describe him?

1159
00:46:37.380 --> 00:46:39.020
He's like a stapler.

1160
00:46:39.660 --> 00:46:40.840
Do I need a stapler?

1161
00:46:41.060 --> 00:46:41.380
Yes.

1162
00:46:42.120 --> 00:46:44.640
But I'm still the one that has to push it down.

1163
00:46:44.880 --> 00:46:45.400
I did.

1164
00:46:45.400 --> 00:46:47.980
I improvised that because I was trying to think of like, what?

1165
00:46:48.160 --> 00:46:50.200
Like, how does Angela see Oscar?

1166
00:46:50.200 --> 00:46:54.900
And he's just like, he's necessary, but he's a, he ends up being more work for her.

1167
00:46:55.040 --> 00:46:55.380
Yes.

1168
00:46:55.380 --> 00:46:56.160
But he's annoying.

1169
00:46:56.500 --> 00:47:03.500
What was so clear to me when I was watching them was this accounting dynamic had already started.

1170
00:47:03.800 --> 00:47:03.960
Yeah.

1171
00:47:03.960 --> 00:47:10.460
This little pod that you guys form, this little trio, it was there from the very beginning.

1172
00:47:10.960 --> 00:47:15.680
Also, I do have to point fans, all of the deleted scenes are fantastic.

1173
00:47:15.940 --> 00:47:18.540
Rainn Wilson has some talking heads.

1174
00:47:18.540 --> 00:47:26.380
There is a long, extended, deleted scene talking head of him talking to Ken Quapis, who played the documentarian for us.

1175
00:47:26.460 --> 00:47:31.500
He would ask us the questions, our director, all about where the boom microphone was.

1176
00:47:32.280 --> 00:47:35.900
Rainn, as Dwight, was very distracted by the boom.

1177
00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:36.900
Oh, my God.

1178
00:47:36.920 --> 00:47:39.420
And he was saying, I really need you to move that.

1179
00:47:39.720 --> 00:47:41.500
Ken says, we can put it in your blind spot.

1180
00:47:41.500 --> 00:47:43.500
And he says something like, I don't have a blind spot.

1181
00:47:43.500 --> 00:47:46.440
I've been trained, so it's especially distracting for me.

1182
00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:48.340
You need to understand that I'm highly trained.

1183
00:47:48.480 --> 00:47:55.680
And it's just this little, like, non-presentational conversation between Dwight and the documentarian about how to be miked.

1184
00:47:55.740 --> 00:47:56.860
I just want to say one thing.

1185
00:47:57.080 --> 00:48:01.540
So many great things that were established in the pilot that you go on to see for years to come.

1186
00:48:01.760 --> 00:48:04.720
Of course, one of the big ones is the Jim versus Dwight.

1187
00:48:04.720 --> 00:48:09.520
And, like, sort of their battle, right, and the pranks that Jim is going to do on Dwight.

1188
00:48:09.640 --> 00:48:17.700
And I love that it just started out so simply with Dwight pushing all of Jim's papers back to his desk and then Jim making the pencil wall.

1189
00:48:18.240 --> 00:48:19.440
It's just ridiculous.

1190
00:48:19.960 --> 00:48:21.880
Well, that was borrowed from the British show.

1191
00:48:22.040 --> 00:48:24.140
In the British show, they have the same desk war.

1192
00:48:24.320 --> 00:48:27.540
But instead, the Jim character piles up a bunch of boxes.

1193
00:48:27.720 --> 00:48:30.060
He makes a box wall between the desks.

1194
00:48:30.580 --> 00:48:32.840
And we did that when we filmed the pilot.

1195
00:48:33.040 --> 00:48:35.220
We had Jim make a box wall.

1196
00:48:35.540 --> 00:48:39.380
And, in fact, you can see it in the background of conference room scenes.

1197
00:48:39.520 --> 00:48:42.480
You can see a bunch of boxes piled up on Jim's desk.

1198
00:48:42.580 --> 00:48:44.660
My lady, I'm going to go back and look.

1199
00:48:45.160 --> 00:48:51.680
Later, when we got picked up, we reshot that scene and turned it into a little pencil thing.

1200
00:48:51.880 --> 00:48:57.800
And you can also see that Rain's hair is a little bit different in the pencil scene compared to the rest of the pilot.

1201
00:48:57.920 --> 00:48:58.600
It's not as poofy.

1202
00:48:58.820 --> 00:48:59.460
That's right.

1203
00:48:59.580 --> 00:48:59.780
Yeah.

1204
00:48:59.780 --> 00:49:00.340
Exactly.

1205
00:49:00.780 --> 00:49:06.840
Rain, as Dwight, went through poofy hair phase, which looks like they used a little miniature, like, rolling brush to blow dry his hair.

1206
00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:09.840
And then he went through flat, parted-down-the-middle phase.

1207
00:49:10.020 --> 00:49:12.400
Well, Rain created the hairstyle for Dwight.

1208
00:49:12.460 --> 00:49:18.080
And he said before that it is based on the hairstyle that he wore when he was a 16-year-old boy.

1209
00:49:18.640 --> 00:49:20.680
And so he did his own hair for the pilot.

1210
00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:22.200
That makes me laugh.

1211
00:49:22.380 --> 00:49:24.040
I've seen those photos of Rain.

1212
00:49:24.320 --> 00:49:25.060
They're crazy.

1213
00:49:25.480 --> 00:49:26.940
You wore your own clothes in the pilot.

1214
00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:28.880
I wore my own clothes in the pilot.

1215
00:49:28.880 --> 00:49:29.500
All right.

1216
00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:37.800
So the last sort of thing I think we need to talk about is this big prank on Pam, quote-unquote, the scene where Michael fake fires her.

1217
00:49:37.800 --> 00:49:41.580
Well, he's so excited because Jim has had a very successful prank.

1218
00:49:41.800 --> 00:49:44.220
And Michael just – he wants to be in the spotlight.

1219
00:49:44.220 --> 00:49:45.500
He wants to be the funny guy.

1220
00:49:45.500 --> 00:49:49.940
So he's going to show off for Ryan the Temp, and he's going to fake fire Pam.

1221
00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:53.220
And it, of course, goes horribly wrong, and she ends up in tears.

1222
00:49:53.420 --> 00:49:57.600
And we shot that scene probably 30 times.

1223
00:49:57.780 --> 00:49:58.060
Wow.

1224
00:49:58.060 --> 00:50:03.540
And at the beginning of the scene, you can see everyone in the background kind of getting ready to leave for the day.

1225
00:50:04.500 --> 00:50:07.980
And all those people had to wait for hours.

1226
00:50:08.380 --> 00:50:11.800
They would have to start in the background, and then we would do this whole scene.

1227
00:50:11.800 --> 00:50:13.140
Oh, I know, Jenna.

1228
00:50:13.260 --> 00:50:13.720
I know.

1229
00:50:13.940 --> 00:50:14.980
I was there.

1230
00:50:14.980 --> 00:50:19.240
I had to grab my jacket, my coat, and my purse, put them down, my coat and my purse.

1231
00:50:19.360 --> 00:50:26.560
I think of any scene in the entire history of The Office, this is the one that I've done the most because we did about 30 takes of it that day.

1232
00:50:26.640 --> 00:50:28.540
But it was also my audition scene.

1233
00:50:28.780 --> 00:50:30.840
This was one of my audition scenes.

1234
00:50:30.880 --> 00:50:34.680
And so I auditioned for months for this role.

1235
00:50:34.900 --> 00:50:39.440
I did that scene one time when I auditioned for the role of Pam, and I didn't get it.

1236
00:50:40.700 --> 00:50:43.800
This is such a crazy, crazy bit of trivia.

1237
00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:44.360
Yes, yes.

1238
00:50:44.540 --> 00:50:47.320
Your first audition for The Office was for the role of Pam.

1239
00:50:47.360 --> 00:50:50.660
Was for the role of Pam, and I wore a pink sweater.

1240
00:50:51.020 --> 00:50:51.300
What?

1241
00:50:51.440 --> 00:50:51.580
Yes.

1242
00:50:51.580 --> 00:50:52.680
We both had the same instinct.

1243
00:50:52.720 --> 00:50:53.320
I wore a pink sweater.

1244
00:50:53.320 --> 00:50:53.960
Pam wore his pink sweater.

1245
00:50:54.100 --> 00:51:00.640
I wore a pink sweater and my hair down, and I was told to look not too fancy, so I didn't do too much with my hair and makeup.

1246
00:51:00.860 --> 00:51:05.500
And I went in, and I did the scene, and it was for a room full of people, the producers.

1247
00:51:05.980 --> 00:51:11.440
And when we got to the point where Michael fake fires you, and you start to cry, and you call him a jerk.

1248
00:51:11.620 --> 00:51:11.860
Yeah.

1249
00:51:11.860 --> 00:51:15.260
When I called him a jerk, everyone started to laugh.

1250
00:51:15.400 --> 00:51:17.120
And I thought, I did something wrong.

1251
00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:19.540
I don't think they're supposed to laugh at this moment.

1252
00:51:19.700 --> 00:51:27.840
And of course, Jenna, you were so brilliant because you brought such a vulnerability to Pam that even though this job sucks and she hates it, she needs it.

1253
00:51:27.940 --> 00:51:29.620
She needs this crappy job.

1254
00:51:29.720 --> 00:51:29.880
Yeah.

1255
00:51:29.880 --> 00:51:32.640
And you really felt her in that moment.

1256
00:51:32.820 --> 00:51:34.580
You just, my heart broke for her.

